\pard\sl363\slmult0\par
Preface \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 TINY BASIC provides the most fundamental of those functions normally attributed to the high-level programming language called BASIC. It is specifically designed for a microcomputer with minimal memory. The TINY BASIC interpreter program requires only 2K bytes of storage. Thus, an Evaluation Kit with 4K of RAM can accomodate modest (about 100 statements in length) TINY BASIC programs. \par
\par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 TINY BASIC is perhaps the best language for the beginning microcomputer programmer. It is easily learned, and elementary application programs may \fs19 be \fs22 developed quickly. For the more experienced programmer, TINY forms the kernel of a system whose facilities may \fs19 be \fs22 extended indefinitely by the addition of machine-language subroutines .(limited only by the amount of memory \'b7which is available).\par
 \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 TINY packs a significant amount of processing capability within 2K bytes. For example, it includes its own line editor, and it provides a rich assortment of error messages to the user. However, clearly one cannot expect certain features which are normally available only in 8K systems. For example, TINY does not do floating-point arithmetic. (Its numeric capability is limited to integers in the range -32768 to +32767.) It cannot directly handle arrays or alphanumeric strings. (On the other hand, each of these (and other) advanced facilities may be added via a machine-language extension). In addition, one must recognize that economies in memory space used were achieved at the expense of processing speed. \par
\par
Generally, then, TINY BASIC may be considered as a good "budget" high-level language for a user with a comparable microcomputer setup. Although TINY is quite slow and is of limited capability, it can act as the nucleus of a system whose sophistication may \fs19 be \fs22 indefinitely extended. \par
\par
\pard\qc\fs23 10-1 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb\qc\fs22 COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\pard INTRODUCTION \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 We assume that you are already familiar with section III of the Evaluation Kit Manual which explains the functions available from the resident utility program UT4. UT4 permanently resides in memory locations 8000-8IFF. After it is given control (via the RESET, RUN U, CR or LF sequence), it types its prompt character, an asterisk, indicating that it is awaiting your input. Each of your input lines (terminated with a CR) is interpreted and executed by UT4. After dis\-posing of your input command, UT4 indicates that it is ready for new. input by typing another \f1\fs29 * \f0\fs22 prompt. \par
\par
One important function of UT4 is to permit you to load an arbitrary sequence of hexadecimal digits (a machine language program) into an arbitrary area in memory and then to invoke this program (transfer control to it; run it) via the appropriate $P command. When your program completes its computation, it may relinquish control back to UT4 by executing a C08039 instruction (a long branch to the location labeled START on p.3-l6), provided all registers used by UT4 have the values they had when UT4 exitedf Under these conditions, a user program halt (or exit) would be signified by a new \f1\fs25 * \f0\fs22 UT4 prompt. \par
\par
COSMAC \fs20 2K \fs22 TINY BASIC is a program which must be loaded into the lowest \fs20 2K \fs22 bytes of memory (locations 0000-07FF). A hexadecimal listing of the program and loading instructions for it appear in Appendix A. After TINY BASIC is made resident, control is transferred to it using the proper $P UT4 command (see Appendix A). Once it receives control, TINY BASIC delivers its prompt character, a colon, and awaits your input. Each time after it has properly disposed of an input line (terminated with a carriage return -CR), TINY BASIC again types its : prompt\f2 .\par
 \par
---------\par
note: in particular, P should be 5. \par
\f0 10-2\par
\pard\par
\pard\pagebb\sl371\slmult0 CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit \tab\tab COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 If an input line does not begin with a number, TINY BASIC immediately interprets it and executes it. (The line is palled a statement.) If the line begins with a number (normally followed by a statement), then TINY BASIC merely stores it, in the proper position, in an area of memory where the user program (a sequence of statements ordered by statement number) is \f3\fs15 ~~led. \f0\fs22 If the statement number is the same as one already existing in this area, then the new statement replaces the old one. Thus, you load a TINY BASIC program by entering a sequence of statements (one per line), each preceded by a unique statement number. The program must have at least one END statement in it. \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 After your program has been loaded, you can run it by typing a RUN command (equivalent to the $P command to UT4). TINY BASIC will then interpret and execute your program's statments, in order, following the rules discussed \f1\fs20 in \f0\fs22 subsequent sections. When an END statement is encountered during execution, control will \fs19 be \fs22 passed back to TINY BASIC's "enter" mode, and another : prompt will be issued. \par
\f1\fs16\par
\f0\fs22 Note that TINY BASIC assembles statements which begin with numbers into the program area in memory without any further analysis. Errors are detected only when execution is attempted. If an entered line consists only of a line number, it \i is \i0 considered a deletion. The previously inserted statement with the same line number is erased. Note also that 0 is not a valid line number. Blanks within a line have no significance to TINY. All spaces, until the first non-numeric character, are totally ignored. After that, however, blanks are preserved \i in \i0 the memory copy of the statement (i.e., each blank character occupies one byte). \par
\pard\f1\fs16\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\f0\fs22 NUMBERS\par
\par
A number is any sequence of decimal digits optionally preceded by a sign. If no sign is present, the number is assumed positive. Since TINY BASIC stores all numbers internally as l6-bit signed integers, positive values may run from 0 to 32767 (2 \super\up9\fs16 15 \nosupersub\up0\fs21 _1) \fs22 and negative values may run from \f1\fs19 -1 \f0\fs22 to -32768 (_2 \super\up10\fs16 15\nosupersub\up0 ). \par
\par
\pard\sl373\slmult0\fs22 10-3 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb\fs23 CDP18S020 \fs22 Evaluation Kit \tab\fs23 COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\fs22 VARIABLES\par
A variable is any single capital letter (A-Z). Each possible variable \fs23 is \fs22 assigned a unique two-byte location in memory. The value of the variable is the contents of that location --i.e., a number in the range -32768 to +32767. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 EXPRESSIONS \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\fs21 An expression \fs22 is a combination of one or more numbers or variables, joined by operators and possibly grouped by parenthesis pairs. The permissible operators are: \par
\par
\pard\sl363\slmult0\fs23 + \tab\fs22 addition \par
-\tab subtraction \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0\f1\fs30 * \tab\f0\fs22 multiplication \par
/ \tab division \par
\par
Whenever TINY BASIC encounters an expression within a statement (during its execution) it evaluates the expression --combining the numbers and the values of the variables, using the indicated operations. The exact disposition of the final computed value depends on the type of statement. This is discussed further later. \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 Internal sub-expressions within parentheses are evaluated first. Usually parentheses make clear the order in which operations are to be performed. However, if there is ambiguity because parentheses are absent, TINY gives precedence to multiplication and division over addition and subtraction. Thus, in evaluating \par
\tab B-14*C \par
the multiplication is performed first. In cases involving two operators of equal precedence, evaluation would proceed from left to right. \fs19 An \fs22 expression may be optionally preceded by a sign.\par
 \par
\pard\fs23 10-4\par
\par
\pard\pagebb CDP18S020 \fs22 Evaluation Kit    \fs23 COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0\fs22 Note that during the evaluation of an expression, all intermediate values, and the final value, are truncated --using the lowest 16 bits of the results. That is, expressions are evaluated modulo \fs21 2\super\up10\fs16 16 \nosupersub\up0\bullet  \fs22 TINY BASIC makes no attempt to discover arithmetic overflow conditions, except that an attempt to divide by zero results in an error stop. \par
\par
\pard\sl376\slmult0 The following are some examples of valid expressions: \par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 (Note that a single variable or number is also an expression.) \par
\fs20 A \par
\fs22 123 \par
1+2-3 \par
B-14*C \par
\pard\sl378\slmult0 (A+B)/(C+D) \par
-128/(-32768+(I*I\'bb \fs25\par
\pard\sl376\slmult0\fs22 ((((Q))))\par
\par
The following are some examples of \par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 -4096 \par
15*4096 \par
32768/8 \par
30720+30720 \par
\pard\sl360\slmult0 because any number in the range 32768 to 65535 (2**15 to 2**16) has a sign bit of 1 (making it negative), so that it is actually treated by TINY BASIC as if 65536 (2 \super\up9\fs16 16 \nosupersub\up0 ) \fs22 were subtracted from it.\par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 THE RND FUNCTION \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 TINY BASIC includes the ability to generate a positive pseudo-random number in a specified range. Whenever it encounters the form \par
RND (expression 1, expression 2) \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 during execution of a statement, TINY generates a random number in the range from the value of expression 1 to the value of expression 2, inclusive. The resulting number may be used as would any other number. In particular, the above form may itself be used within another expression. If the arguments are invalid, an error stop may result. \par
\par
\pard 10-5 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard THE RND FUNCTION (cont'd) \f2\par
\par
\pard\sl373\slmult0 RND (1,100) RND (A,B) \par
are valid RND functions (assuming 0<A<B). \par
\f0\par
\pard STATEMENT TYPES \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 A statement normally begins with a keyword, such as PRINT or GOTO, indicating the type of statement. The interpretation of the remainder of the statement depends on this keyword. In some cases, a short form of the key word is also acceptable -- for example, PR instead of PRINT. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 REM STATEMENT \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 Following the keyword REM (for remark or comment) any sequence of characters may appear. This statement is ignored by TINY BASIC. It is used to permit you to intersperse arbitrary comments or remarks within your program. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 END STATEMENT \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\fs19 END \fs22 must be the last statement executed in a program. It is used to halt execution and return to TINY BASIC's "enter" mode. There may be as many END statements \f1\fs19 in \f0\fs22 a program as needed. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 LET STATEMENT \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 This statement has the form \par
\pard\fi100\sl371\slmult0 LET variable \fs30 = \fs22 expression Alternatively, the keyWord LET may be omitted entirely. Execution of this statement assigns the value of the expression to the variable. The following are valid LET statements: \par
\par
\pard\qj LET A=B+C \par
I = I + 1\par
J = 0\par
LET \fs23 Q = \fs22 RND (5,33)\fs21\par
\par
\pard\fs22 10-6 \par
\pard\qj  \par
\pard\pagebb\fs23 CDP1BS020 Evaluation Kit \tab COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard\fs22 IF STATEMENT \par
\par
This statement has the \fs23 form \par
\pard\fi3\sl371\slmult0\fs22 IF expressionl relation expression2 THEN statement \par
\par
The keyword THEN may be omitted entirely. Execution of this statement evaluates the two expressions and compares them according to the relation specified. If the condition specified is TRUE, \f3\fs15  \f0\fs22 then the associated state\-ment is executed. Otherwise, the associated statement is skipped. The permissible relational operators are as follows: \par
\par
\pard\sl338\slmult0 = equal \par
< less than. \par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 > greater than \par
<= less than or equal (not greater) \par
\fs23 >= \fs22 greater than or equal (not less) \par
<> or >< not equal (greater than or less than) \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 The associated \tab statement may be any other valid TINY BASIC statement including, \f1\fs20 in \f0\fs22 particular, another IF statement. The following are some valid IF statements: \par
IF I>25 THEN END \par
IF A>B IF B>C I=I+1 \par
(The last statement increments I only if B is between C and A.) \par
\par
\pard\sl363\slmult0 TRANSFERS OF CONTROL \par
\par
TINY BASIC normally executes statements in a program in statement number order. The following statements may be used to alter this flow: \par
\par
(a) \tab GOTO expression \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 The subsequent statement executed is the one whose line number equals the value of the expression. Note that this permits you to compute the line number of the next statement on the basis of program parameters during execution. The following are some valid GOTO statements: \par
\pard\sl366\slmult0\par
GOTO 100\par
GO TO 200 \f1\fs21 + \f0\fs22 1*10 \par
\par
\pard\fs23 10-7 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb CDP18S020 \fs22 Evaluation Kit \fs23 COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
(b) G\fs22 OSUB expression \par
\par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 This statement executes exactly as does the GOTO statement, except that in addition TINY records (remembers) the statement number of the following statement (the one which would have 'been executed next, had the branch not taken place). \par
\par
(c) RETURN\par
\f1\fs17\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\f0\fs22 This statement (which also has the short form RET) executes by transferring control back to the statement whose number was last recorded as the result of the execution of a GOSUB. This last-recorded statement number is also forgotten. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 SUBROUTINE NESTING \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 A subroutine is a sub-program which is normally evoked in \fs19 two \fs22 or more places within a main program. Rather than duplicate the statements of the sub-program in several places, it appears only once. It is written so that it exits with a RETURN statement. It is evoked at any point in a program by a GOSUB statement which transfers control to it. \par
\par
Whenever one subroutine calls another subroutine (termed subroutine "nesting"), an additional "return-statement-number" is recorded. These are stored in order, so that every RETURN jumps back to the statement following the GOSUB which called it. Subroutines may be nested to any depth, limited only by the amount of user program memory remaining. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 PRINT STATEMENT \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 This statement has the form \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 PRINT printlist \par
\par
where printlist is a succession of one or more items to be printed separated by either commas or semicolons. The acceptable short form for PRINT is PR. Each print item may be either an expression or a character string enclosed in quotes. In the first case the value of the expression is typed. In the second case the character string is printed verbatim. No spaces are generated \par
\pard\fi514\sl371\slmult0 10-8\fs23\par
\pard\par
\pard\pagebb\f2\fs22 CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard\sl360\slmult1 between the printouts of items separated by semicolons in the PRINT statement. \par
\par
On the other hand, the printout of an item, preceded by a comma in the PRINT statement, begins at the next "tab setting. Tabs are automatically set everyeight character spaces. Thus, \par
PRINT 1,2,3 prints as \par
1\tab 2\tab 3 \par
while PRINT 1;2;3 \i  \i0 prints as \par
123 \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 Commas and semicolons, character strings and expressions may be mixed in one PRINT statement in any manner. \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\par
Normally, the execution of a PRINT statement terminates with the generation of a carriage return and line feed to begin a new line. However, if the PRINT statement ends with a comma or semicolon, then the CR-LF sequence is suppressed, permitting subsequent PRINT statements to output on the same line or permitting an input message (see INPUT, next) to appear on the same line as previous output. \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 The following are valid PRINT statement examples: \par
\par
PRINT "A=";A,"B+C=";B+C\i\par
\i0 PR (generates a blank line) \par
PRI (prints the value of variable I) \par
PRINT 1,','.Q*P;",".R/42; \par
\par
INPUT STATEMENT \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0\f0\fs23 This statement has the form \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 INPUT inputlist \par
where inputlist is a succession of one or more variables separated by commas. The acceptable short form for INPUT is IN. Normally, execution of this statement begins with the typing of a question mark prompt indicating that TINY is expecting the user to type in data. The user should respond by typing in a line of one or more expressions separated by commas and terminated with a carriage return. Each input expression is evaluated and assigned to its associated variable in the INPUT statement. \par
\par
\pard\qc 10-9 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb\sl368\slmult0\fs22 If the number of requested variables in the inputlist is not satisfied by the number of expressions in the user's input line, a new? prompt will be issued asking for more input information. If the number of expressions in the user's input line is greater than the number \i\fs23 ot \i0\fs22 requested variables, then those input expressions not requested are saved internally and used to satisfy subsequent INPUT requests. Thus, before a \fs21 ? \fs22 prompt is issued during execution of an INPUT instruction, TINY first checks to see if any saved expressions exist. If so, then these are used first -to satisfy some or all of the variables requesting values. Only when no saved data exists is the \fs21 ? \fs22 prompt issued. The user is cautioned to use the latter property of the INPUT statement with care. \par
\par
\pard\sl360\slmult1 Example: Suppose statement INPUT X,Y,Z is executed, and the user responds by typing A,C,B. The results are the same as if X=A, Y=C and Z=B had been executed. Note that commas are required in the user's input line only to avoid ambiguity. If he had entered ACB, the same results would have occurred. On the other hand, an input line of +1 -3 +6 0 in response to INPUT A,B,C,D will result in A being given the value 58 and a new \fs19 ? \fs22 prompt issued for values for B, C and D. \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0\par
SYSTEM CONTROL STATEMENTS \par
\par
\pard\sl360\slmult1 The statements listed below are normally not included as part of a program. That is, they are normally entered without line numbers: \par
(a) NEW\par
Execution of this statement clears the program area in memory. It is used before entering a new program. \par
(b)  RUN \par
Begin program execution at the first (lowest) line number. Note: If RUN is followed by a comma followed by a sequence of one or more expressions (separated with commas), then the expression list is treated as an initial input line --which will be scanned first whenever INPUT statements are executed. (See discussion of INPUT statement.) \par
\par
(c) LIST \par
LIST expression \par
LIST expression, expression\par
\par
\pard\sl380\slmult0 10-10 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb\fs23 COSMAC TINY BASIC CDP18S020 \fs22 Evaluation Kit \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 SYSTEM CONTROL STATEMENTS (cont'd) \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 (c) \tab (cont'd) \fs23 The \fs22 LIST statement causes part or all of a stored user program to \fs20 be \fs22 printed. If no parameters are given, the whole program is listed. A single expression parameter is evaluated to a line number. If the line exists, it is printed. If both parameters are given, all lines with numbers in the range specified are printed. \par
\par
10-11\par
\par
\pard\pagebb\f2 SUMMARY OF COSMAC TINY BASIC REPERTOIRE \par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 The following should serve as your short form guide to the facilities offered by TINY BASIC. Characters enclosed in brackets [ ] are optional and may be omitted.\par
\par
FORM OF STATEMENT \tab\tab BRIEF EXPANATION OF EXECUTION\par
\par
\pard REM any comment\tab\tab\tab\cf0 Ignored.\par
\par
END\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Halt execution and return to I"enter" mode.\par
\par
[LET\} variable = expression\tab\tab Assign the value of the expression to the variable\par
\par
IF expr rel expr [THEN] statement\tab If the relation between the values of the expressions is TRUE,\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab  execute the statement, otherwise, skip it.\par
\par
GOTO expression\tab\tab\tab Jump to the statement whose number is the expression's value.\par
\par
GOSUB expression\tab\tab\tab Save the statement number of the next statement\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab in sequence. Then execute a GOTO.\par
\par
RET[URN]\tab\tab\tab\tab Jump to the last saved statement number\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab (see GOSUB) and "unsave" this number.\par
\par
PR[INT] printlist\tab\tab\tab Type the items in the printlist. Type values\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab of expressions. Type quoted strings verbatim.\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Horizontal TAB on comma.\par
\par
IN[PUT] inputlist\tab\tab\tab Read and evaluate expressions from the keyboard\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab and assign them in order to the variables\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab specified in the inputlist.\par
\par
NEW\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Clear the program area.\par
\par
RUN[,expression sequence]\tab\tab Start execution at first statement. (Save\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab the expression sequence to satisfy\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab subsequent INPUT's.)\par
\par
LIST[expression],[expression]\tab\tab Print entire program, or one selected line,\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab or a range of lines.\par
\par
where:\par
\par
\f0 number = -32768 to +32767; variable = single capital letter.\par
\f2\par
expression = one or more numbers or variables (possibly grouped by parentheses) joined by operators +,-,*, or /.\par
\par
relations are =,>,<,<=,>=,<>, or >< \par
\par
printlist = one or more expressions or quoted strings separated by commas or semicolons. \par
\par
inputlist = one or more variables separated by commas. \par
\par
expression sequence = one or more expressions separated by commas. \par
\par
\pard\sl258\slmult0\qj NOTE: \tab The RND(exprl,expr2) function generates a positive random number in the range between the values of the expressions. This function may be used anywhere in place of a number. \par
\par
10-12 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb CDP18S020 COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard IMMEDIATE EXECUTION VS. PROGRAM MODE \par
\par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 One important use of the immediate execution mode (entering a statement without a line number) is to permit line-at-a-time testing. LET, IF and PRINT can be demonstrated this way. Due to the way TINY BASIC buffers its input lines, the INPUT statement cannot be directly executed for more than one variable at a time, and if the following statement is typed in without a line number, \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 INPUT A,B,C \par
the value of B will be copied to A, and only one value (for C) will be requested from the console/terminal. Similarly, the statement, \par
INPUT X,1,Y,2,Z,3 \par
will execute directly (loading.X,Y, and Z with the values 1,2,3), requesting no input, but with a line number, in a program, this statement will produce an error stop after requesting one value. \par
\pard\fi147\sl371\slmult0\par
\pard\sl373\slmult0 Clearly there is no point to executing REM or END in the immediate mode. Furthermore, GOSUB and RETURN are normally meant for the program mode. On the other hand, an immediate GOTO has the same effect as if RUN were typed, but execution may begin at other than the program's first statement.\par
 \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 Similarly, the stored program should not contain a NEW statement (self destruct!), and a stored RUN statement will be equivalent to a GOTO to the first statement. On the other hand, a LIST statement may be included as part of a program and used for printing large text strings, such as instructions to the operator. \par
\pard\qc 10-13 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\par
\pard PROGRAMMING EXAMPLES \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 The following two simple programs are designed to give you examples of TINY BASIC in action. The first uses most of the statements in TINY's repertoire. The second demonstrates particularly the use of subroutines. REMarks are omitted from the listings to keep them short. Instead, each program is accompanied by a detailed explanation of its functioning. (It should be emphasized that omission of comments is generally bad documentation practice, but it suits our present objectives.) Each program can be entered in a few minutes. It is recommended that you run both of them to gain. experience with the system. \par
\par
\pard I. Arithmetic Drill Program \par
\par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 This program generates a random sequence of arithmetic problems. After the program prints the problem, you respond with your solution. The program tells you whether your answer was correct or not (providing the right answer in the latter case) and then proceeds to generate a new problem, and so on. \par
\par
Stepping through the program listed below: first, three random numbers are generated. The value of F (1 to 4) will be used to decide whether this will be an add, subtract, multiply or divide problem. The range of Possible values for the arguments A and B was chosen to prevent the Possiblity of overflow under two conditions: First, 181*181 is still less than 32767. Second, division by zero is prevented. Because TINY BASIC discards division remainders, the fourth state\-ment is included to keep the division problems interesting. It says: If this is a division problem where the quotient would ordinarily come out as zero (true for many of the A,B combinations that might be generated), arbitrarily increase the size of the dividend (to a maximum of 18100 in this case) to make the problem non-trivial. Statement 50 begins the presentation of the problem to the user by printing an encouraging message followed by the value of the first argument. Notice that the final semicolon keeps the printer on the same line without advancing the carriage further. \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 Statement 60 does a four-way branch based on the value of F (the arithmetic function selected). Thus, control passes next to one of the following statement numbers: 70, 100, 130 or 160. Each of these statements begins a short sequence which prints the sign for the arithmetic operation and then computes the proper \par
10-14 \par
\par
\pard\pagebb I. Arithmetic Drill Program (cont'd) \par
\par
\pard\sl360\slmult1 function, placing the result in c. (Notice the final semicolons again, \par
in the PRINT statements.) No matter which path is taken, control passes next to \par
statement 180, which prints the second argument value followed by an = sign. \par
The presentation of the problem to the user is now complete, and the INPUT \par
statement at 190 delivers a ? prompt on the same print line and reads the \par
user's answer into D. Statement 200, congratulates the user on a correct answer, \par
while 210 points out that his answer was incorrect and provides him with the \par
proper result. The commas at the end of both PRINT statements here again inhibit \par
a new line from starting, but they space over to the next tab setting, where a new \par
problem is posed as a result of the loop (at 220) back to the top. \par
\fs23\par
\pard\sl248\slmult0\fs24 10 A=RND (1,181) \par
20 B=RND (1,181)\par
30 F=RND (1,4) \par
40 IF F=4 IF A/B<1 A=A*100 \par
50 PRINT "TRY THIS ONE: ";A;\par
60 60 TO 40+F*30 \par
70 PRINT "+";\par
80 C=A+B \par
90 GO TO 180 \par
100 PRING "-";\par
110 C=A-B: \par
120 GO TO 180 \par
\pard 130 PRINT "*"; \par
140 C=A*B\par
\pard\sl248\slmult0 150 GO TO 180 \par
160 PRINT "/";\par
170 C=A/B \par
180 PRINT B;" =" ; \par
190 INPUT D\par
200 IF D=C PRINT "RIGHT!". \par
210 IF D <> C PRINT "WRONG. CORRECT ANSWER IS ";C,\par
220 GO TO 10 \par
\f0\fs26\par
\pard\sl360\slmult1\fs22 Notice that an END statement is not present here --contrary to earlier advice. \par
The nature of this program is such that TINY will never go past the last statement. \par
The program as written loops endlessly, and only under these conditions is the \par
omission of an END permissible. \par
Running this program should give you some practice in learning how TINY divides.\par
