\pard\sl368\slmult0\fs22 II. Geometric Print Pattern Program \par
\par
This program is designed to print three identical, trapezoidal patterns across the page, each filled with repeated imprints of the same numeric digit. The user can specify which digit is to fill each trapezoid and, for all three, the number of characters across its top, the slope of its sides (positive or negative) and its height. He can also specify the spacing between the patterns on the page. \par
\par
Since the printer prints line-by-line, the program prints the pattern in a scanning mode. Every line consists of-a sequence of three identical segments, and each segment contains 0 spaces followed by E identical digits followed by \fs21 D \fs22 spaces again. The values of 0 and E vary from line to line. For each new line, \par
\pard\sl360\slmult0\fs25 D \fs22 is decremented by a value I (positive or negative) and E is incremented by \f1\fs20 2*F \f0\fs22 (to keep the pattern symmetrical). \par
\par
\pard\sl368\slmult0 To analyze the program listed below, let us begin by identifying its subroutines. Reading from the bottom up, the subroutine from 250 to 280 prints the digit N, M times across (notice the semicolon). Similarly, the subroutine from 210 to 240 prints a sequence of M spaces. Finally, the subroutine from 140 to 200\fs20  \fs22 prints 0 spaces followed by E digits (all N) followed again by 0 spaces. Notice that this subroutine calls the other two. \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0\par
\f2 The main part of the program runs from 10 to 130. First, the program initializes a counter J for the number of lines which have been printed. Then it reads (from the user) initial values for A to E, I and L (the total number of lines to be printed). A,B and C should be single digits. O,E and L must be > 0. Each of the three sequences 30-40, 50-60, and 70-80 prints one segment of a line using the digit specified by the user. 85 starts a new line. 90 and 100 advance D and E as explained earlier, and 110-120 decide whether or not a sufficient number of lines have yet been printed. If not, a new line is started. \f0\par
10-16\par
\par
\pard\pagebb\f2 CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit COSMAC TINY BASIC \par
\pard\pagebb\qc\par
\pard GEOMETRIC PRINT PATTERN PROGRAM \par
\f1\fs18\par
\f2\fs24 10 J=0 \par
20 INPUT A,B,C,D,E,I,L \par
30 N=A \par
\pard\sl248\slmult0 40 GOSUB 140 \par
\pard 50 N=B \par
\pard\sl248\slmult0 60 GOSUB 140 \par
\pard 70 N=C \par
\pard\sl248\slmult0 80 GOSUB 140 \par
85 PRINT \par
90 D=D-I\par
\pard 100 E=E+2+I \par
110 J=J+1 \par
120 IF J<>L GO TO 30 \par
130 END\par
\pard\sl248\slmult0 140 M=D \par
150 GOSUB 210 \par
160 M=E \par
170 GOSUB 250 \par
\pard 180 M=D \par
\pard\sl248\slmult0 190 GOSUB 210 \par
200 RETUrN \par
\pard 210 PRINT " ";\par
220 M=M-1 \par
230 IF M>0 GOT0 210 \par
240 RETURN \par
250 PRINT N;\par
260 M=M-1 \par
270 IF M>0 GOTO 250 \par
280 RETURN \par
\f3\fs17\par
\pard\sl360\slmult1\f0\fs22 For this program to run properly the values of D and E should not become \par
too small. Nor should they be so large as to require excessive line length. \par
The inital values should obey the following relations: 3 (E+2D) \fs20 < \fs22 maximum line \par
width; If I<0, E>2|I|(L-l); If I>0, D>I(L-1). \par
\pard\par
\pard\qc\fs23 10-17 \par
