\pard\pagebb\f2\fs22 APPENDIX B \par
\par
\pard REGISTER ALLOCATIONS \par
\pard\sl366\slmult0 Registers R0 and R1 are not used by TINY BASIC in any way. In addition, the program makes no reference to Q or EFl,2,3 or 4. All character I/O is funnelled through a vector near the beginning of the program. The user may request the performance of INP or OUT instructions as part of the BASIC program, but these are up to the user's discretion. \par
\par
\pard\sl376\slmult0 The other registers used by TINY are as follows: \par
2 Control stack pointer.\par
3 Inner interpreter Program Counter.\par
4 Call linkage PC. \par
5 Return linkage PC. \par
\pard\sl371\slmult0 6 Top of control stack; -address of caller. Also holds branch address.\par
7 Byte Fetch PC. \par
8 Temporary work register. Receives second argument in USR call \par
9 OUter interpreter Program Counter. ~address of next IL opcode. \par
A l6-bit accumulator and work register. Contains third argument of USR calls, and part of response from USR calls. \par
B BASIC Pointer. Points to next token.\par
C Timing subroutine in  Terminal I/O. \par
D Workspace memory pointer. = Expression Stack Pointer in USR calls. \par
E Subroutine linkage temporary and Terminal timing constant. \par
F Temporary work register. \par
\par
\pard\sl376\slmult0 Machine language subroutines called via the USR function have the free use of R0,R1,R8,RA,RD,RF. \par
